00:00:06.553 --> 00:00:08.008
Flooding Stress in Rice

00:00:08.553 --> 00:00:11.920
Plant Responses to Environmental Stresses and Global Climate Change:PLSCS 4100 
Hi and welcome to our video for our&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:11.920 --> 00:00:15.920
course plant responses to environmental&nbsp;
stresses and global climate change

00:00:18.000 --> 00:00:22.720
Today we'll be talking about flooding stress&nbsp;
in rice. Gabby will introduce us to the topic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:22.720 --> 00:00:27.600
and talk about the N-end rule. then Elise will take&nbsp;
over and discuss how plants adapt to flooding and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:27.600 --> 00:00:33.360
some responses they have. Alice will detail where&nbsp;
plants get the energy to respond to flooding&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:33.360 --> 00:00:37.360
and Yuxing will wrap it up by discussing&nbsp;
how farmers can protect their crops&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:37.360 --> 00:00:43.840
Let's get to it. Today we will be talking about&nbsp;
how plants respond to being drowned. We all know&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:43.840 --> 00:00:48.560
that plants need water but too much water like&nbsp;
in the case of root water logging or partial&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:48.560 --> 00:00:53.360
or complete plant flooding means that they have&nbsp;
limited access to oxygen since they are no longer&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:53.360 --> 00:00:59.120
exposed to as much air. Without oxygen it becomes&nbsp;
difficult for plants to photosynthesize and grow.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:59.680 --> 00:01:04.160
The lack of sufficient oxygen for normal&nbsp;
respiration levels is known as hypoxia.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:04.160 --> 00:01:07.920
While the point at which respiration&nbsp;
is forced to stop completely is anoxia.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:09.040 --> 00:01:14.000
There are two main physical responses that plants&nbsp;
can have to flood stress. One is the snorkel&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:14.000 --> 00:01:19.120
response which as its name implies involves the&nbsp;
plant stretching above the water surface to reach&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:19.120 --> 00:01:24.400
air. Seeds will usually choose this response but&nbsp;
mature plants can also increase their growth rate&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:24.400 --> 00:01:30.160
in order to act as a snorkel. The second response&nbsp;
is called quiescence which is when the plant&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:30.160 --> 00:01:35.600
might slow down its metabolic activities and&nbsp;
grow as much as possible so that it can survive&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:35.600 --> 00:01:40.480
under the water in the hopes that the flood will&nbsp;
pass. You can probably already think of some of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:40.480 --> 00:01:44.640
the pros and cons of each method especially&nbsp;
when it comes to how long a flood can last.

00:01:48.080 --> 00:01:52.720
How can plants tell when their soil is flooded?&nbsp;
The answer to this question follows a pathway&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:52.720 --> 00:01:58.400
called the n-end rule because it takes place&nbsp;
at the N-end of an ERV or ethylene-responsive&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:58.400 --> 00:02:03.680
transcription factor which is a protein that's&nbsp;
dependent on ethylene. In normal conditions&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:03.680 --> 00:02:08.320
oxygen prevents proteins involved in flooding&nbsp;
stress responses from being produced in the plant.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:08.880 --> 00:02:13.920
This is because oxygen allows arginine to bind&nbsp;
to the cysteine already on the protein. And the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:13.920 --> 00:02:20.720
combination of arginine and cysteine marks the ERF&nbsp;
for degradation by a proteasome. Without oxygen&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:20.720 --> 00:02:26.080
the N-end remains unoxidized and is therefore&nbsp;
not marked for degradation. This means that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:26.080 --> 00:02:30.560
it's the lack of oxygen that initiates a plant's&nbsp;
response to flooding rather than the water itself.

00:02:34.960 --> 00:02:38.560
I'm sure you can picture a rice patty&nbsp;
with plants half submerged in water.

00:02:42.240 --> 00:02:46.240
For rice the most common practice is for&nbsp;
growers to germinate the seed elsewhere&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:46.240 --> 00:02:50.000
and plant the seedlings in the field&nbsp;
when they are already established.&nbsp;

00:02:50.000 --> 00:02:54.560
In recent years however a shortage of labor has&nbsp;
caused many rice farmers to seed rice directly in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:54.560 --> 00:02:58.800
the field, so it's important that they have&nbsp;
varieties that can germinate under flooded&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:58.800 --> 00:03:03.920
conditions. Of all the cereal crops rice is the&nbsp;
most tolerant to flooding thanks to a couple&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:03.920 --> 00:03:09.440
physiological aspects. First of all rice seeds&nbsp;
store most of their energy in starch rather than&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:09.440 --> 00:03:14.960
fatty acids. Rice seedlings germinate with all&nbsp;
the enzymes that they need to break down starch.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:14.960 --> 00:03:20.080
One of the most important is alpha amylase&nbsp;
an enzyme that helps break down starch into&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:20.080 --> 00:03:24.400
carbohydrates that are easily fermentable&nbsp;
and can be used in anaerobic respiration.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:25.040 --> 00:03:27.680
This helps them gain the energy&nbsp;
they need to germinate and grow.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:28.480 --> 00:03:33.040
One amazing feature of rice and other flood&nbsp;
tolerant plants is that it has something called&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:33.040 --> 00:03:38.160
aerenchyma in the roots. These are special&nbsp;
channels in the parenchymal layer that travel&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:38.160 --> 00:03:42.720
through the root and all the way up the shoot.&nbsp;
This lets air travel from the top of the plant&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:42.720 --> 00:03:48.480
all the way to the bottom like a snorkel. In order&nbsp;
to prevent this air from being lost out the sides&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:48.480 --> 00:03:53.920
of the plant there is a waterproof barrier made&nbsp;
of lignin and suberin on the outside of the root.

00:03:56.960 --> 00:04:01.680
Another result of submergence underwater&nbsp;
is the accumulation of the gaseous hormone&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:01.680 --> 00:04:07.200
ethylene which builds up because gases diffuse&nbsp;
slowly through water compared to air. Plants can&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:07.200 --> 00:04:13.280
respond to ethylene in a variety of ways depending&nbsp;
on genetic background, cellular energy, O2 levels&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:13.280 --> 00:04:18.720
and other factors. In some flooding tolerant&nbsp;
varieties of rice increased ethylene leads to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:18.720 --> 00:04:26.160
expression of ERF SUB1, a gene that suppresses&nbsp;
growth to initiate the quiescence response. In&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:26.160 --> 00:04:31.600
other species ethylene leads to the expression of&nbsp;
snorkel genes which cause the plant to elongate.

00:04:34.800 --> 00:04:40.800
There are two ways that the growing plant can&nbsp;
elongate: cell division and cell expansion. Since&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:40.800 --> 00:04:45.760
cell division requires more energy the plant&nbsp;
will favor cell expansion whenever possible.

00:04:49.040 --> 00:04:52.720
The snorkel genes lead to&nbsp;
down regulation of peroxidase&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:52.720 --> 00:04:56.880
which is involved in holding cell walls&nbsp;
tightly together which restricts elongation&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:57.680 --> 00:05:02.880
and the up regulation of expansions. These are&nbsp;
proteins that loosen cell walls by breaking the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:02.880 --> 00:05:09.600
bonds between cellulose microfibrils. This&nbsp;
allows the cells to expand. Snorkel genes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:09.600 --> 00:05:15.440
also up regulate gibberellin, another plant&nbsp;
hormone, that increases production of our&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:15.440 --> 00:05:20.560
old friend alpha amylase which helps provide the&nbsp;
plant with the energy it needs for rapid growth.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:21.360 --> 00:05:26.320
While the shoots grow fast root growth is&nbsp;
repressed to save energy for the shoots.

00:05:29.760 --> 00:05:34.320
Responding to flooding takes a lot of energy and&nbsp;
it becomes difficult to produce energy when your&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:34.320 --> 00:05:40.000
roots are under water. Plants, like animals, use&nbsp;
cellular respiration to produce energy in the form&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:40.000 --> 00:05:46.880
of ATP. Cellular respiration includes glycolysis,&nbsp;
the Krebs cycle, and oxidative phosphorylation. When&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:46.880 --> 00:05:52.000
there is no oxygen available, plants must switch&nbsp;
to anaerobic respiration. This means energy will&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:52.000 --> 00:05:56.720
be produced via glycolysis and fermentation&nbsp;
instead of oxidative phosphorylation.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:56.720 --> 00:06:01.920
Unfortunately this means the plant will produce&nbsp;
18 times less ATP for each molecule of sugar.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:02.480 --> 00:06:08.000
In addition to producing less energy, fermentation&nbsp;
results in toxic byproducts including ethanol and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:08.000 --> 00:06:12.880
lactate. Although there are mechanisms in place&nbsp;
to minimize the buildup of these compounds.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:13.600 --> 00:06:18.320
Luckily rice has a lot of starch to use&nbsp;
for energy production and it rations its&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:18.320 --> 00:06:22.880
ATP efficiently so that it can survive in&nbsp;
flooded conditions for long periods of time.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:22.880 --> 00:06:26.480
These features of rice set it apart from&nbsp;
other crops that are not flood tolerant

00:06:30.560 --> 00:06:36.800
Under the changeable weather condition now some&nbsp;
areas in the past leaves rainfall now may also&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:36.800 --> 00:06:42.560
encounter the flooding. To the farmers maybe they&nbsp;
didn't plant flooding resistant rice species at&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:42.560 --> 00:06:48.880
the beginning of the season. Then unexpectedly&nbsp;
flooding is coming. In that situation they need&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:48.880 --> 00:06:55.520
some agronomy method to reduce their loss because&nbsp;
they cannot just abandon the plants on their field&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:55.520 --> 00:07:01.840
and replace them with a resistant one. So they&nbsp;
need some crops to make a living. When the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:01.840 --> 00:07:07.600
situation is not so bad they just need some&nbsp;
moderate flooding agronomic measures. Most&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:07.600 --> 00:07:13.600
frequently there are three steps as shown. First&nbsp;
clean the ditches and drain the water in time&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:13.600 --> 00:07:23.200
and then wash the leaves and nourish seedlings by applying &nbsp; top fertilizer outside the roots. However&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:23.200 --> 00:07:29.040
sometimes there is some extreme flooding. There&nbsp;
is a lot of rainfall for a long time. The river&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:29.040 --> 00:07:34.880
and groundwater levels are high so it is hard&nbsp;
to drain the water in time. In this situation&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:34.880 --> 00:07:42.960
the plants need urgent rescue. After the rice is&nbsp;
flooded the ground is seriously damaged and the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:42.960 --> 00:07:50.320
leaves are dead and rotten. And the growth of the&nbsp;
young ears is blocked. In this situation they can&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:50.320 --> 00:07:57.600
mow the seedlings and allow them to regenerate.&nbsp;
Second is pile the soil, third apply fertilizer&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:57.600 --> 00:08:05.520
in timely manner, fourth field management capacity&nbsp;
shallow water layer and appropriate aggregation,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:06.320 --> 00:08:12.320
five pest control. Moisture after&nbsp;
flooding is good for pest hatching.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:12.320 --> 00:08:18.320
Rice has higher resistance to flooding. For&nbsp;
example there is a kind of rice called deep water&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:18.320 --> 00:08:25.280
rice. This rice can grow in the water even deeper&nbsp;
than human bodies height. As long as the leaves&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:25.280 --> 00:08:31.440
can stick out the water they can grow. This is&nbsp;
the reason why we choose rice to start our video.

00:08:35.600 --> 00:08:39.200
Because of climate change it&nbsp;
is important to study flooding&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:39.200 --> 00:08:45.360
stress. It can help develop flood resistant&nbsp;
varieties and they can help farms to reduce&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:45.360 --> 00:08:50.320
their loss. If on their farms the varieties&nbsp;
is not highly resistant to the flooding.

00:08:51.200 --> 00:08:53.840
Thank you for watching our video

